data.content:
This is a known **C# 14 breaking change: `Span<T>` overloads applicable in more scenarios**.

## What happened

In C# 14, new built-in span conversions make `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` a better overload match than `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)` when called on arrays. Your `array.Contains(num)` now binds to the `MemoryExtensions` span overload.

Expression trees **cannot represent calls to span-based methods** (spans are ref structs). So the expression tree compiles fine, but the interpreted expression tree interpreter throws at runtime because it can't handle the span overload.

## How to fix

Force the compiler to pick `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the compiler from choosing the span overload, keeping the expression tree representable. **Option 3** (`Enumerable.Contains(...)`) is the most explicit and least likely to regress in future compiler updates.
